+## Overdue command
+
+CLI-010 to CLI-012 are a draft of 2026-11-05 and are not built. The status note at the top
+of this file applies to them.
+
+### CLI-010: overdue
+
+`ticketdesk overdue` prints one line per overdue ticket, in the order of TKT-019, see also
+TKT-018. Each line is the line that `list` prints for that ticket per CLI-006, so each one
+shows `OVERDUE`. The log path follows CLI-002. The `--log` option goes before the
+subcommand, as for every command, for example `ticketdesk --log desk.jsonl overdue`. With
+no overdue tickets it prints nothing and exits with code 0, as `list` does with no tickets
+and as CLI-008 requires. A missing log file is one such case, see LOG-006.
+
+### CLI-011: overdue takes no arguments
+
+`ticketdesk overdue` takes no argument and no option of its own after the command name. An
+unrecognized argument, such as `ticketdesk overdue extra`, is rejected with a usage error
+on stderr and exit code 2. Nothing is printed to stdout. ticketdesk 0.3.0 rejects
+`ticketdesk list extra` in the same way. The help option `-h` is outside this item.
+
+### CLI-012: overdue on a damaged log
+
+`ticketdesk overdue` and `ticketdesk list` follow LOG-007 and INV-004. A log line that
+cannot be read stops the command with an error that names the file and the line. Nothing
+is skipped and no partial list is printed, because a skipped `closed` event would show a
+closed ticket as overdue. An unknown priority in the log (TKT-003) stops the command in
+the same way, because the due time cannot be computed. Both failures escape as tracebacks
+today, see the TODO under CLI-007, and the overdue commands inherit whatever the user
+decides there.
